<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Wallace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1270–<a href="/years/1305">1305</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Wallace was a Scottish leader in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-war-of-scottish-independence">First War of Scottish Independence</a>. With Andrew Murray he defeated an English army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stirling-bridge">Battle of Stirling Bridge</a> in 1297 and was made Guardian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, but he was beaten by Edward I at Falkirk in 1298. Captured in 1305, he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and executed for treason, and became a lasting Scottish national hero.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-wallace.jpg" alt="William Wallace" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 11th Earl of Buchan, David Stuart Erskine · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WilliamWallaceByDavidofBuchan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>William Wallace</b> defeated an English army at the Battle of Stirling Bridge in 1297 but lost at Falkirk in 1298.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Betrayed and captured near Glasgow, <b>William Wallace</b> was hanged, drawn and quartered at Smithfield.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-23">23 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Sir John de Menteith, a Scottish knight loyal to Edward I, turned <b>William Wallace</b> over to English soldiers at Robroyston, near Glasgow.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Scottish forces under Andrew Moray and <b>William Wallace</b> destroyed an English army under the Earl of Surrey as it crossed the narrow bridge over the River Forth near Stirling.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-11">11 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
